{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b6f29654dff6a33e", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# MOVA: Towards Scalable and Synchronized Video-Audio Generation\narXiv: 2602.08794\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We develop MOVA, a synchronized video-audio generation model with an asymmetric dual-tower architecture that leverages pre-trained video and audio generation models.\n  2. We design a fine-grained audio-video captioning pipeline to produce high-quality bimodal training data at scale.\n  3. By scaling video-audio training, we achieve continuous improvements in synchronization performance across both modalities.\n\nTags: related-work, we develop, we design, by scaling", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d49b9378-f79d-454d-9453-fd71bd8ac44c", "uuid": "4f1b9906-a570-42a2-9f7c-671e086bcfc0", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T12:33:55.592Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# MOVA: Towards Scalable and Synchronized Video-Audio Generation\narXiv: 2602.08794\nAuthors: \nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We develop MOVA, a synchronized video-audio generation model with an asymmetric dual-tower architecture that leverages pre-trained video and audio generation models.\n  2. We design a fine-grained audio-video captioning pipeline to produce high-quality bimodal training data at scale.\n  3. By scaling video-audio training, we achieve continuous improvements in synchronization performance across both modalities.\n\nTags: related-work, we develop, we design, by scaling", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-95fad1da54930bd1", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# NAVA: Native Audio-Visual Alignment for Generation\narXiv: 2605.30073\nAuthors: Longbin Ji∗\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose NAVA, a Native Audio-Visual Alignment framework that formulates joint audio-video generation as context-conditioned native audio-visual alignment to enable precise event-level correspondence modeling.\n  2. We introduce an Align-then-Fuse MMDiT architecture for modality-aware audio-video alignment and efficient collaborative denoising, alongside Timbre-in-Context Conditioning for flexible content-timbre binding.\n  3. Extensive experiments and user studies demonstrate that NAVA significantly outperforms representative baselines in audio-visual synchronization, semantic consistency, visual quality, and timbre controllability.\n\nTags: experiments, we propose, we introduce, extensive experiments", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d49b9378-f79d-454d-9453-fd71bd8ac44c", "uuid": "426bd913-4efa-4c55-a367-054da0e78908", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T12:33:55.824Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# NAVA: Native Audio-Visual Alignment for Generation\narXiv: 2605.30073\nAuthors: Longbin Ji∗\nYear: 2026 | Venue: \nTopic: \nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose NAVA, a Native Audio-Visual Alignment framework that formulates joint audio-video generation as context-conditioned native audio-visual alignment to enable precise event-level correspondence modeling.\n  2. We introduce an Align-then-Fuse MMDiT architecture for modality-aware audio-video alignment and efficient collaborative denoising, alongside Timbre-in-Context Conditioning for flexible content-timbre binding.\n  3. Extensive experiments and user studies demonstrate that NAVA significantly outperforms representative baselines in audio-visual synchronization, semantic consistency, visual quality, and timbre controllability.\n\nTags: experiments, we propose, we introduce, extensive experiments", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8369ff87021e43d4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# Rolling Forcing: Autoregressive Long Video Diffusion in Real Time\narXiv: 2509.25161\nAuthors: Kunhao Liu, Wenbo Hu, Jiale Xu, Ying Shan, Shijian Lu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A rolling-window joint denoising technique that processes multiple frames with progressively increasing noise levels in a single forward pass to enable mutual refinement and real-time latency.\n  2. An attention sink mechanism that caches the key-value states of initial frames as a global context anchor and dynamically adjusts their Rotary Position Embeddings to enhance long-term global consistency.\n  3. An efficient training algorithm that uses non-overlapping windows and conditions on self-generated histories to enable few-step distillation over extended denoising windows while mitigating exposure bias.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d49b9378-f79d-454d-9453-fd71bd8ac44c", "uuid": "83cd8e81-26bf-4570-8bb9-a26ab7e42ab9", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T12:33:56.050Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# Rolling Forcing: Autoregressive Long Video Diffusion in Real Time\narXiv: 2509.25161\nAuthors: Kunhao Liu, Wenbo Hu, Jiale Xu, Ying Shan, Shijian Lu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A rolling-window joint denoising technique that processes multiple frames with progressively increasing noise levels in a single forward pass to enable mutual refinement and real-time latency.\n  2. An attention sink mechanism that caches the key-value states of initial frames as a global context anchor and dynamically adjusts their Rotary Position Embeddings to enhance long-term global consistency.\n  3. An efficient training algorithm that uses non-overlapping windows and conditions on self-generated histories to enable few-step distillation over extended denoising windows while mitigating exposure bias.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-82115a670f269b56", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# One-step Diffusion with Distribution Matching Distillation\narXiv: 2311.18828\nAuthors: Tianwei Yin, Michaël Gharbi, Richard Zhang, Eli Shechtman, Fredo Durand, William T. Freeman, Taesung Park\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces Distribution Matching Distillation (DMD), a procedure to transform a pretrained multi-step diffusion model into a one-step image generator with minimal quality loss.\n  2. Formulates the distillation objective as the minimization of an approximate KL divergence, whose gradient is computed as the difference between two score functions representing the real and fake image distributions.\n  3. Approximates the real and fake score functions using two separate diffusion models, one fixed on real data and the other dynamically trained on generated samples.\n  4. Proposes an adaptive weighting strategy that normalizes the gradient magnitude across different noise levels to stabilize the distribution matching optimization.\n  5. Integrates a regression loss on pre-computed noise-image pairs to match the large-scale structure of the teacher model, which prevents mode collapse and stabilizes training.\n  6. Achieves state-of-the-art few-step generation performance, reaching 2.62 FID on ImageNet 64x64 and 11.49 FID on zero-shot COCO-30k.\n  7. Enables real-time image generation at 20 FPS using FP16 inference, offering a 30x speedup over the base Stable Diffusion model.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d49b9378-f79d-454d-9453-fd71bd8ac44c", "uuid": "d4f75287-a979-4a2e-b595-bd25f686e1ed", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T12:33:56.281Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# One-step Diffusion with Distribution Matching Distillation\narXiv: 2311.18828\nAuthors: Tianwei Yin, Michaël Gharbi, Richard Zhang, Eli Shechtman, Fredo Durand, William T. Freeman, Taesung Park\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: speech_other\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduces Distribution Matching Distillation (DMD), a procedure to transform a pretrained multi-step diffusion model into a one-step image generator with minimal quality loss.\n  2. Formulates the distillation objective as the minimization of an approximate KL divergence, whose gradient is computed as the difference between two score functions representing the real and fake image distributions.\n  3. Approximates the real and fake score functions using two separate diffusion models, one fixed on real data and the other dynamically trained on generated samples.\n  4. Proposes an adaptive weighting strategy that normalizes the gradient magnitude across different noise levels to stabilize the distribution matching optimization.\n  5. Integrates a regression loss on pre-computed noise-image pairs to match the large-scale structure of the teacher model, which prevents mode collapse and stabilizes training.\n  6. Achieves state-of-the-art few-step generation performance, reaching 2.62 FID on ImageNet 64x64 and 11.49 FID on zero-shot COCO-30k.\n  7. Enables real-time image generation at 20 FPS using FP16 inference, offering a 30x speedup over the base Stable Diffusion model.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
