{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b7454f02b2c7ec73", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1098\tViTPose: simple vision transformer baselines for human pose estimation.\n1099\t\n1100\tIn NeurIPS,\n1101\t\n1102\tCited by: §3.1.\n1103\t\n1104\t- Xu et al. (2026)\n1105\tZ. Xu, J. Ma, Z. Wang, Z. Peng, J. Liang, and J. Li\n1106\t\n1107\tMoCha: end-to-end video character replacement without structural guidance.\n1108\t\n1109\tIn arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.08587,\n1110\t\n1111\tCited by: §1,\n1112\t§2.1,\n1113\t§4.1,\n1114\tTable 1,\n1115\tTable 3.\n1116\t\n1117\t- Yan et al. (2026)\n1118\tW. Yan, F. Guo, Z. Yang, and J. Tang\n1119\t\n1120\tSCAIL-2: unifying controlled character animation with end-to-end in-context conditioning.\n1121\t\n1122\tIn arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.10804,\n1123\t\n1124\tCited by: §4.1,\n1125\tTable 1,\n1126\tTable 2,\n1127\tTable 3.\n1128\t\n1129\t- Yang et al. (2024)\n1130\tZ. Yang, J. Teng, W. Zheng, M. Ding, S. Huang, J. Xu, Y. Yang, W. Chen, X. Dai, et al.\n1131\t\n1132\tCogVideoX: text-to-video diffusion models with an expert transformer.\n1133\t\n1134\tIn arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.06072,\n1135\t\n1136\tCited by: §1.\n1137\t\n1138\t- Yin et al. (2024a)\n1139\tT. Yin, M. Gharbi, T. Park, R. Zhang, E. Shechtman, F. Durand, and W. T. Freeman\n1140\t\n1141\tImproved distribution matching distillation for fast image synthesis.\n1142\t\n1143\tIn NeurIPS,\n1144\t\n1145\tCited by: §2.4.\n1146\t\n1147\t- Yin et al. (2024b)\n1148\tT. Yin, M. Gharbi, R. Zhang, E. Shechtman, F. Durand, W. T. Freeman, and T. Park\n1149\t\n1150\tOne-step diffusion with distribution matching distillation.\n1151\t\n1152\tIn CVPR,\n1153\t\n1154\tCited by: §1,\n1155\t§2.4.\n1156\t\n1157\t- Yin et al. (2025)\n1158\tT. Yin, Q. Zhang, R. Zhang, W. T. Freeman, F. Durand, E. Shechtman, and X. Huang\n1159\t\n1160\tFrom slow bidirectional to fast autoregressive video diffusion models.\n1161\t\n1162\tIn CVPR,\n1163\t\n1164\tCited by: §2.4.\n1165\t\n1166\tSupplementary Material \n1167\t\n1168\t## 6 Qualitative Ablation\n1169\t\n1170\tFigure 6 complements the quantitative ablation in the main paper by visualizing the effect of each component of Feature-RoPE Decomposition over one-minute generations. The ablated variants exhibit increasing identity drift and visual artifacts in later segments, whereas the full model remains more consistent. Together with Table 5, these results support the roles of bounded coordinates, reference re-anchoring, and the adaptive sink block in reducing long-horizon drift.\n1171\t\n1172\tFigure 6: Qualitative ablation on Feature-RoPE Decomposition. Frames are sampled every 10 seconds from one-minute generations. Removing any component leads to visible identity drift and artifacts in later segments, whereas the full model remains more consistent throughout the sequence. Zoom in for details.\n1173\t\n1174\t## 7 KV-Cached Streaming Inference\n1175\t\n1176\tAlgorithm 1 details the blockwise inference procedure used by Stages 2 and 3. The reference cache persists throughout generation, source keys and values are temporary, and completed target blocks are committed to the clean-history cache.\n1177\t\n1178\tAlgorithm 1 KV-Cached Streaming Inference\n1179\t\n1180\t 0:\n1181\t \n1182\t \n1183\t  Reference tokens RR; source blocks {Si}i=0N−1\\{S_{i}\\}_{i=0}^{N-1}; reference region ℛ\\mathcal{R}; history ℋ\\mathcal{H} (sink + rolling)\n1184\t\n1185\t 1:\n1186\t \n1187\t \n1188\t  Prefill reference: forward RR once and write its key/value tensors into ℛ\\mathcal{R} {never evicted}\n1189\t\n1190\t 2:\n1191\t \n1192\t \n1193\t  for i=0i=0 to N−1N-1 do\n1194\t\n1195\t 3:\n1196\t \n1197\t \n1198\t   Prefill source: forward SiS_{i} at the current slot and temporarily cache its key/value tensors\n1199\t\n1200\t 4:\n1201\t \n1202\t \n1203\t   Denoise: iteratively denoise BiB_{i} in read-only mode, attending to ℛ\\mathcal{R}, ℋ\\mathcal{H}, and SiS_{i}\n1204\t\n1205\t 5:\n1206\t \n1207\t \n1208\t   Commit: forward the denoised BiB_{i} as clean context, and append its unrotated keys and values to the sink region if i=0i=0, or to the rolling region otherwise\n1209\t\n1210\t 6:\n1211\t \n1212\t \n1213\t   Shift: once the rolling region is full, evict its oldest block, shift the retained blocks, and reapply RoPE to their cached keys at bounded local coordinates\n1214\t\n1215\t 7:\n1216\t \n1217\t \n1218\t   Rollback: discard the temporary key/value tensors of SiS_{i}\n1219\t\n1220\t 8:\n1221\t \n1222\t \n1223\t  end for\n1224\t\n1225\t 9:\n1226\t \n1227\t \n1228\t  return Generated blocks {Bi}i=0N−1\\{B_{i}\\}_{i=0}^{N-1}\n1229\t\n1230\t## 8 User Study\n1231\t\n1232\tWe conducted a blinded user study with 30 participants. The study compared UniSwap with four video-replacement baselines, each paired with Seed-VC following the cascade protocol in Table 1. Each participant evaluated anonymized outputs from all five methods on four source clips and rated appearance identity, voice identity, lip synchronization, and naturalness on a five-point Likert scale. Method order was randomized independently for each participant.\n1233\t\n1234\tTable 6: User-study results. Ratings are mean scores on a five-point Likert scale.\n1235\t\n1236\tMethod\n1237\tAppearance ID ↑\\uparrow\n1238\tVoice ID ↑\\uparrow\n1239\tLip Sync ↑\\uparrow\n1240\tNaturalness ↑\\uparrow |\n1241\t\n1242\tWan-Animate + Seed-VC\n1243\t | 3.85\n1244\t | 4.04\n1245\t | 3.42\n1246\t | 3.77 |\n1247\t\n1248\tSCAIL-2 + Seed-VC\n1249\t | 4.03\n1250\t | 4.05\n1251\t | 3.67\n1252\t | 3.85 |\n1253\t\n1254\tMoCha + Seed-VC\n1255\t | 3.91\n1256\t | 4.08\n1257\t | 3.54\n1258\t | 3.89 |\n1259\t\n1260\tHunyuanCustom + Seed-VC\n1261\t | 3.64\n1262\t | 3.98\n1263\t | 3.28\n1264\t | 3.61 |\n1265\t\n1266\tUniSwap\n1267\t | 4.16\n1268\t | 3.87\n1269\t | 4.11\n1270\t | 3.96 |\n1271\t\n1272\tUniSwap receives the highest ratings for appearance identity, lip synchronization, and naturalness.\n1273\t\n1274\t## 9 Additional Qualitative Results\n1275\t\n1276\tWe provide additional qualitative results for both short and long source videos. In all figures, each example contains a reference image and reference voice clip, a source video and its audio, and the joint audio-video output produced by UniSwap. The red and blue waveforms denote the source and generated audio, respectively. These examples complement the quantitative evaluation in the main paper by covering diverse identities, poses, gestures, clothing, backgrounds, and recording conditions.\n1277\t\n1278\t## 9.1 Short-Video Results\n1279\t\n1280\tFigures 7 and 8 present 16 additional short-video examples. Across these cases, UniSwap transfers the appearance specified by the reference image while retaining the source composition, body motion, and facial activity. The examples include cross-gender replacement, varied camera framing, substantial hand and upper-body motion, and both simple and cluttered backgrounds. The corresponding generated-audio waveforms are shown alongside the visual outputs to illustrate that the two modalities are produced jointly for the full source sequence.\n1281\t\n1282\tFigure 7: Additional qualitative results on short videos (Part I). For each example, the left column provides the reference image and reference voice, while the right column shows sampled frames and the waveform of the source video/audio followed by the UniSwap output. Red waveforms indicate source audio and blue waveforms indicate generated audio. UniSwap changes the visual and vocal identity according to the references while preserving the source scene composition and motion.\n1283\t\n1284\tFigure 8: Additional qualitative results on short videos (Part II). The examples follow the layout of Fig. 7 and cover additional identities, viewpoints, gestures, and backgrounds. Red and blue denote the source- and generated-audio waveforms, respectively. The generated frames adopt the reference appearance while following the pose, expression, framing, and scene content of the source video.\n1285\t\n1286\t## 9.2 Long-Video Results\n1287\t\n1288\tFigure 9 shows three additional one-minute generations sampled at 10-second intervals. The output identity remains visually consistent from the beginning to the end of each sequence despite continuous autoregressive generation. At the same time, the outputs preserve the source background, camera framing, and time-varying facial and upper-body motion. These results provide further qualitative evidence that the bounded cache coordinates and persistent identity context used by UniSwap mitigate long-horizon identity drift.\n1289\t\n1290\tFigure 9: Additional qualitative results on one-minute videos. Frames are sampled every 10 seconds from 0 to 60 seconds. Each row group shows the reference image and voice, the source video/audio (red waveform), and the UniSwap output (blue waveform). Across all three sequences, the generated character remains consistent with the reference identity throughout the minute while retaining the source scene and temporal performance.\n1291\t\n1292\t## 10 Limitations\n1293\t\n1294\tUniSwap currently targets single-speaker talking videos; multi-speaker scenes, occlusions, and complex interactions remain challenging. Facial expressions are driven automatically by the audio condition rather than controlled explicitly, so the current model does not support independent expression editing or arbitrary user-specified expression control.\n1295\t\n1296\t## 11 Broader Impact\n1297\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "d49b9378-f79d-454d-9453-fd71bd8ac44c", "uuid": "1b0f656f-8813-430d-9521-e896bd9e9ad3", "timestamp": "2026-08-13T12:36:47.152Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/uniswap_text.txt", "content": "ViTPose: simple vision transformer baselines for human pose estimation.\n\nIn NeurIPS,\n\nCited by: §3.1.\n\n- Xu et al. (2026)\nZ. Xu, J. Ma, Z. Wang, Z. Peng, J. Liang, and J. Li\n\nMoCha: end-to-end video character replacement without structural guidance.\n\nIn arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.08587,\n\nCited by: §1,\n§2.1,\n§4.1,\nTable 1,\nTable 3.\n\n- Yan et al. (2026)\nW. Yan, F. Guo, Z. Yang, and J. Tang\n\nSCAIL-2: unifying controlled character animation with end-to-end in-context conditioning.\n\nIn arXiv preprint arXiv:2606.10804,\n\nCited by: §4.1,\nTable 1,\nTable 2,\nTable 3.\n\n- Yang et al. (2024)\nZ. Yang, J. Teng, W. Zheng, M. Ding, S. Huang, J. Xu, Y. Yang, W. Chen, X. Dai, et al.\n\nCogVideoX: text-to-video diffusion models with an expert transformer.\n\nIn arXiv preprint arXiv:2408.06072,\n\nCited by: §1.\n\n- Yin et al. (2024a)\nT. Yin, M. Gharbi, T. Park, R. Zhang, E. Shechtman, F. Durand, and W. T. Freeman\n\nImproved distribution matching distillation for fast image synthesis.\n\nIn NeurIPS,\n\nCited by: §2.4.\n\n- Yin et al. (2024b)\nT. Yin, M. Gharbi, R. Zhang, E. Shechtman, F. Durand, W. T. Freeman, and T. Park\n\nOne-step diffusion with distribution matching distillation.\n\nIn CVPR,\n\nCited by: §1,\n§2.4.\n\n- Yin et al. (2025)\nT. Yin, Q. Zhang, R. Zhang, W. T. Freeman, F. Durand, E. Shechtman, and X. Huang\n\nFrom slow bidirectional to fast autoregressive video diffusion models.\n\nIn CVPR,\n\nCited by: §2.4.\n\nSupplementary Material \n\n## 6 Qualitative Ablation\n\nFigure 6 complements the quantitative ablation in the main paper by visualizing the effect of each component of Feature-RoPE Decomposition over one-minute generations. The ablated variants exhibit increasing identity drift and visual artifacts in later segments, whereas the full model remains more consistent. Together with Table 5, these results support the roles of bounded coordinates, reference re-anchoring, and the adaptive sink block in reducing long-horizon drift.\n\nFigure 6: Qualitative ablation on Feature-RoPE Decomposition. Frames are sampled every 10 seconds from one-minute generations. Removing any component leads to visible identity drift and artifacts in later segments, whereas the full model remains more consistent throughout the sequence. Zoom in for details.\n\n## 7 KV-Cached Streaming Inference\n\nAlgorithm 1 details the blockwise inference procedure used by Stages 2 and 3. The reference cache persists throughout generation, source keys and values are temporary, and completed target blocks are committed to the clean-history cache.\n\nAlgorithm 1 KV-Cached Streaming Inference\n\n 0:\n \n \n  Reference tokens RR; source blocks {Si}i=0N−1\\{S_{i}\\}_{i=0}^{N-1}; reference region ℛ\\mathcal{R}; history ℋ\\mathcal{H} (sink + rolling)\n\n 1:\n \n \n  Prefill reference: forward RR once and write its key/value tensors into ℛ\\mathcal{R} {never evicted}\n\n 2:\n \n \n  for i=0i=0 to N−1N-1 do\n\n 3:\n \n \n   Prefill source: forward SiS_{i} at the current slot and temporarily cache its key/value tensors\n\n 4:\n \n \n   Denoise: iteratively denoise BiB_{i} in read-only mode, attending to ℛ\\mathcal{R}, ℋ\\mathcal{H}, and SiS_{i}\n\n 5:\n \n \n   Commit: forward the denoised BiB_{i} as clean context, and append its unrotated keys and values to the sink region if i=0i=0, or to the rolling region otherwise\n\n 6:\n \n \n   Shift: once the rolling region is full, evict its oldest block, shift the retained blocks, and reapply RoPE to their cached keys at bounded local coordinates\n\n 7:\n \n \n   Rollback: discard the temporary key/value tensors of SiS_{i}\n\n 8:\n \n \n  end for\n\n 9:\n \n \n  return Generated blocks {Bi}i=0N−1\\{B_{i}\\}_{i=0}^{N-1}\n\n## 8 User Study\n\nWe conducted a blinded user study with 30 participants. The study compared UniSwap with four video-replacement baselines, each paired with Seed-VC following the cascade protocol in Table 1. Each participant evaluated anonymized outputs from all five methods on four source clips and rated appearance identity, voice identity, lip synchronization, and naturalness on a five-point Likert scale. Method order was randomized independently for each participant.\n\nTable 6: User-study results. Ratings are mean scores on a five-point Likert scale.\n\nMethod\nAppearance ID ↑\\uparrow\nVoice ID ↑\\uparrow\nLip Sync ↑\\uparrow\nNaturalness ↑\\uparrow |\n\nWan-Animate + Seed-VC\n | 3.85\n | 4.04\n | 3.42\n | 3.77 |\n\nSCAIL-2 + Seed-VC\n | 4.03\n | 4.05\n | 3.67\n | 3.85 |\n\nMoCha + Seed-VC\n | 3.91\n | 4.08\n | 3.54\n | 3.89 |\n\nHunyuanCustom + Seed-VC\n | 3.64\n | 3.98\n | 3.28\n | 3.61 |\n\nUniSwap\n | 4.16\n | 3.87\n | 4.11\n | 3.96 |\n\nUniSwap receives the highest ratings for appearance identity, lip synchronization, and naturalness.\n\n## 9 Additional Qualitative Results\n\nWe provide additional qualitative results for both short and long source videos. In all figures, each example contains a reference image and reference voice clip, a source video and its audio, and the joint audio-video output produced by UniSwap. The red and blue waveforms denote the source and generated audio, respectively. These examples complement the quantitative evaluation in the main paper by covering diverse identities, poses, gestures, clothing, backgrounds, and recording conditions.\n\n## 9.1 Short-Video Results\n\nFigures 7 and 8 present 16 additional short-video examples. Across these cases, UniSwap transfers the appearance specified by the reference image while retaining the source composition, body motion, and facial activity. The examples include cross-gender replacement, varied camera framing, substantial hand and upper-body motion, and both simple and cluttered backgrounds. The corresponding generated-audio waveforms are shown alongside the visual outputs to illustrate that the two modalities are produced jointly for the full source sequence.\n\nFigure 7: Additional qualitative results on short videos (Part I). For each example, the left column provides the reference image and reference voice, while the right column shows sampled frames and the waveform of the source video/audio followed by the UniSwap output. Red waveforms indicate source audio and blue waveforms indicate generated audio. UniSwap changes the visual and vocal identity according to the references while preserving the source scene composition and motion.\n\nFigure 8: Additional qualitative results on short videos (Part II). The examples follow the layout of Fig. 7 and cover additional identities, viewpoints, gestures, and backgrounds. Red and blue denote the source- and generated-audio waveforms, respectively. The generated frames adopt the reference appearance while following the pose, expression, framing, and scene content of the source video.\n\n## 9.2 Long-Video Results\n\nFigure 9 shows three additional one-minute generations sampled at 10-second intervals. The output identity remains visually consistent from the beginning to the end of each sequence despite continuous autoregressive generation. At the same time, the outputs preserve the source background, camera framing, and time-varying facial and upper-body motion. These results provide further qualitative evidence that the bounded cache coordinates and persistent identity context used by UniSwap mitigate long-horizon identity drift.\n\nFigure 9: Additional qualitative results on one-minute videos. Frames are sampled every 10 seconds from 0 to 60 seconds. Each row group shows the reference image and voice, the source video/audio (red waveform), and the UniSwap output (blue waveform). Across all three sequences, the generated character remains consistent with the reference identity throughout the minute while retaining the source scene and temporal performance.\n\n## 10 Limitations\n\nUniSwap currently targets single-speaker talking videos; multi-speaker scenes, occlusions, and complex interactions remain challenging. Facial expressions are driven automatically by the audio condition rather than controlled explicitly, so the current model does not support independent expression editing or arbitrary user-specified expression control.\n\n## 11 Broader Impact\n", "numLines": 200, "startLine": 1098, "totalLines": 1357}}}
